                    round_name_r3:          `Design & Budget`,
                    round_name_r4:          `Defend & Stress-Test`,

                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the behaviour, not the awareness gap`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Resist the instinct to run a third awareness campaign — the last two cost <strong>CA$6.5M</strong> and moved behaviour <em>under 1%</em>. People already know recycling is good and still don't do it 72% of the time, so this is a <strong>behaviour-change problem, not an awareness gap</strong>. Pick the sharpest priority behaviour — the package is recycled only <strong>28%</strong> of the time, a repeated, low-observability habit. Then name the real barrier between intention and action: <em>friction and habit</em>, not a lack of knowledge or caring. Set measurable baselines, because the board will judge behaviour, not impressions.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Select the SHIFT levers matched to each barrier`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Work the SHIFT framework — <strong>S</strong>ocial influence, <strong>H</strong>abit, <strong>I</strong>ndividual self, <strong>F</strong>eelings, <strong>T</strong>angibility — and match a lever to each behaviour's barrier, not one lever for all three. Recycling is low-observability and repeated, so <em>habit</em> (defaults, on-pack cues, friction reduction) beats a social norm nobody sees at the curb. Food waste is private and hard to feel, so <em>tangibility</em> (concrete CA$ and meals saved) beats another reminder. The plant-forward switch is a one-off identity choice, so <em>individual self</em> beats a habit prompt. Guard against a norm that accidentally signals the wrong behaviour is normal.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Design the campaign & budget for conversion`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Allocate the fixed <strong>CA$4.0M</strong> across on-pack, in-store, app/loyalty, digital and partnership. Broad digital is cheap per impression and converts almost nobody — reach is a vanity number here. The board is judging <strong>cost per conversion</strong> and <strong>90-day retention</strong>, so weight the channels that actually change and sustain behaviour. Design a measurement that can <em>prove</em> uptake — a control group plus loyalty-card data and packaging returns — and fund the recurring prompts and defaults that make the new behaviour stick past launch.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Defend the plan & stress-test for backfire`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Pitch the CMO and the board member who killed the last two campaigns. Lead with the spine that wins: <strong>behaviour → matched levers → measured conversion and 90-day retention</strong>, not reach. Keep the on-pack claim <em>specific and substantiated</em> — anything that reads as “recycling this saves the planet” is greenwashing waiting for an audit. Name the biggest backfire risk and commit to a real <strong>evidence gate</strong>: scale only if attributable uptake and 90-day retention clear the bar against the control group.`,

                    r1_frame_label:         `How do you reframe the problem the board is funding?`,
                    frame_behaviour:        `A behaviour-change problem — people know recycling matters and still don't do it 72% of the time`,
                    frame_awareness:        `An awareness problem — consumers still need to be told sustainability matters`,
                    frame_product:          `A product problem — redesign the packaging and the behaviour follows`,
