                    warn_conviction_range:  `Conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_frame_right:   `<strong>That's the right reframe.</strong> This is a behaviour-change problem, not an awareness gap — people already know recycling is good and still don't do it 72% of the time. Now you can target the barrier between intention and action instead of buying more eyeballs.`,
                    coach_r1_awareness:     `<strong>You're funding a third awareness campaign.</strong> That's exactly what the last two CA$6.5M efforts did — and they moved behaviour under 1%. Awareness was never the missing ingredient. What barrier between intention and action are you actually removing?`,
                    coach_r1_product:       `<strong>Redesigning the package is a different project — and a different budget.</strong> Your flagship pack is already recyclable; it's recycled only 28% of the time. The gap is behaviour, not engineering. Reframe it as the behaviour you need people to perform.`,
                    coach_r1_all:           `<strong>Three behaviours, one identical message, and you'll move none of them.</strong> Recycling, food waste and the plant-forward switch have completely different barriers. Pick the sharpest priority first — the package stuck at 28% — and match the intervention to it.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You framed it as behaviour change AND named the real barrier as friction and habit. That's the intention–action gap at the heart of SHIFT — now you can design defaults and cues that remove the friction rather than shouting louder.`,
                    coach_r1_knowledge:     `<strong>“They don't know recycling is good” is the awareness fallacy.</strong> They know. The 28% rate persists despite knowledge. The barrier is friction and habit — the easy default path doesn't lead to the behaviour. Information won't fix that.`,
                    coach_r1_caring:        `<strong>People care more than their behaviour shows — that's the intention–action gap.</strong> Caring isn't the binding constraint here; friction and habit are. Two CA$6.5M campaigns already proved that making people care more didn't make them act.`,
                    coach_r1_lowrigour:     `<strong>Without measurable baselines you're flying on vibes.</strong> The board killed the last two campaigns for unprovable claims. Set the behavioural baselines you intend to move now, or you can't prove you moved anything.`,

                    coach_r2_rec_social:    `<strong>A social norm for recycling misfires.</strong> Putting out the recycling at the curb is low-observability — your neighbours don't see it, so “most households recycle” has nothing to anchor to. Recycling is a repeated habit: defaults, on-pack cues and friction reduction move it, not a norm.`,
                    coach_r2_rec_feelings:  `<strong>An emotional film is another awareness campaign in disguise.</strong> Feelings don't fix a low-observability, repeated habit. Recycling needs the easy default path to lead to the bin — that's Habit, not Feelings.`,
                    coach_r2_wst_social:    `<strong>Careful — that norm can backfire.</strong> “Most people waste less than you” can quietly signal that wasting food is normal, or shame the people already doing well. Food waste is private and hard to feel: make the impact tangible — CA$ and meals saved — rather than normative.`,
                    coach_r2_wst_aware:     `<strong>Another reminder that waste is bad won't move private behaviour.</strong> People know. What closes the intention–action gap is tangibility — concrete, personal, immediate feedback on what they saved this month.`,
                    coach_r2_swt_social:    `<strong>A visible movement helps the switch — but it isn't the sharpest lever.</strong> The plant-forward switch is a one-off, identity-laden choice; connecting it to the consumer's own identity and values (Individual self) lands harder than a generic norm.`,
                    coach_r2_swt_habit:     `<strong>A re-ordering prompt fits a repeated action, not a one-off identity choice.</strong> The switch to plant-forward is about who the consumer sees themselves as. Reach the Individual self, not the loyalty-app habit loop.`,
                    coach_r2_allmatched:    `<strong>Every lever matched to its barrier — this is SHIFT done right.</strong> Habit for the repeated, invisible recycle; tangibility for the private, hard-to-feel waste; individual self for the identity-laden switch. No one-size-fits-all message in sight.`,
                    coach_r2_onelever:      `<strong>You've pulled the same kind of lever for behaviours that don't share a barrier.</strong> SHIFT's discipline is matching the lever to each behaviour's barrier. Which one is genuinely social, which is a habit-and-friction problem, and which is about identity?`,
                    coach_r2_backfire:      `<strong>You've left a normative message unguarded.</strong> A norm that signals the wrong behaviour is normal can move it the wrong way. Stress-test every norm before it ships, or it backfires.`,

                    coach_r3_reach:         `<strong>You've poured the budget into broad digital for the reach.</strong> You'll reach 5 million households and convert almost none — reach is a vanity number here. The board is judging cost per conversion and 90-day retention. Would you rather touch 5 million people once or change behaviours that stick?`,
                    coach_r3_conversionmix: `<strong>That's a conversion-first mix.</strong> Weighting app/loyalty, partnership and on-pack over broad digital buys behaviours changed, not impressions counted — and it sets up the 90-day retention the board actually wants.`,
                    coach_r3_control:       `<strong>A control group makes the uptake attributable.</strong> Loyalty-card data and packaging returns against a control let you prove the campaign caused the change — exactly the measurement the last two campaigns lacked.`,
                    coach_r3_selfreport:    `<strong>A post-campaign survey asks people if they changed — and they over-report.</strong> Self-report isn't attributable behaviour. The board killed the last two campaigns for unprovable claims; design observation, not opinion.`,
                    coach_r3_impressions:   `<strong>Impressions and ad recall count who saw the campaign, not who changed.</strong> That's exactly the eyeballs metric the board rejected. If you can't observe the behaviour, you can't claim it.`,
                    coach_r3_retention:     `<strong>You've funded the habit mechanics that make it stick.</strong> Recurring prompts and persistent defaults are what keep the behaviour alive at 90 days — without them you buy a launch spike that decays the week the media stops.`,
                    coach_r3_noretention:   `<strong>Almost nothing into 90-day habit mechanics.</strong> You'll get a launch spike that reverts to baseline once the prompts stop. Durable change needs recurring prompts and defaults that persist — fund them.`,

                    coach_r4_conversion:    `<strong>That's the spine that wins this board.</strong> Behaviour → matched levers → measured conversion and 90-day retention answers exactly why this beats the awareness campaigns they killed.`,
                    coach_r4_reach:         `<strong>“We'll reach 5 million households” is the pitch they've heard twice.</strong> Reach is the vanity number that moved behaviour under 1%. Defend cost per conversion and retention, not the size of the audience.`,
                    coach_r4_emotion:       `<strong>An inspiring planet story is another awareness campaign.</strong> Emotion without matched levers and measurement is exactly what failed. Lead with behaviour, levers, conversion and retention.`,
                    coach_r4_bold:          `<strong>“Recycling this saves the planet” is greenwashing waiting for an audit.</strong> A retailer audit or watchdog reads that as an over-claim, and your sustainability campaign becomes a reputational story for the wrong reasons. Tangibility means an honest, specific benefit.`,
                    coach_r4_vague:         `<strong>“Eco-friendly, green, natural” is unsubstantiated feel-good language.</strong> Vague claims are the classic greenwashing trap and won't survive the retailer's audit. Say the specific, provable benefit instead.`,
                    coach_r4_gated:         `<strong>An honest pitch, a substantiated claim and a real evidence gate.</strong> Scaling only when attributable uptake and 90-day retention clear the bar vs control is exactly how you avoid bankrolling a spike that decays. The board can fund this.`,
                    coach_r4_launchgate:    `<strong>A launch-week spike is not proof.</strong> Scaling on momentum is how the last initiative decayed once attention moved on. Gate the scale-up on attributable 90-day retention, not the opening week.`,
                    coach_r4_nogate:        `<strong>No gate, full national rollout — that's how you scale a failure.</strong> Without an evidence gate you commit the whole budget before you know the behaviour stuck. Scale only what the control group proves.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
